+
+# Scope and evidence
+
+Static review of main at `30d158c7ead3252c5c0aa398e8ab1819ffa9d75b`, after
+PRs #957 and #958 merged. The review covered core transitions, shell scheduling,
+process/resource/workspace ownership, analyzer/target/plan/review, persistence,
+progress, configuration and Python release tooling. Three independent domain
+reviews supplemented the coordinator's source inspection. No implementation,
+runtime tests, benchmarks or Lean proofs were executed during this audit.
+Existing tests were inspected as contracts and proposed validation targets.
+
+The software-design-philosophy criterion is net reduction in knowledge and
+coordination. The user explicitly permits breaking compatibility. That permits
+removing obsolete Rust constructors; it does not make current error rejection,
+platform behavior or independently observable lifecycle evidence obsolete.
+The catalog overview, architecture, runtime, selection, sessions and related
+historical audits were consulted before tracing current writers and readers.
+
+# Assessment
+
+The inspected boundaries satisfy six of eight diagnostics: **7.5/10**, using
+1.25 points per evidenced row. This is a scoped qualitative assessment, not a
+repository-wide measurement. Both findings are small maintenance improvements;
+no production failure was reproduced.
+
+| Diagnostic | Result | Evidence or required improvement |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Modules have describable purposes | Pass | Core transitions, shell I/O, discovery, selection and artifact readers have distinct roles. |
+| Interfaces are simpler than implementations | Pass | Typed requests, managed ownership and artifact readers hide substantial machinery. |
+| Implementation changes remain local | Needs work | Historical Top/TopRange compatibility requires duplicated construction and normalization across parser, plan and preview. Normalize once into one Top variant. |
+| Interface comments describe intent | Pass | Process drain, cancellation and workspace ownership comments identify evidence and lifetime limits. |
+| Design is reviewed with code | Pass | Recent maintenance records and this audit discuss alternatives and preservation obligations. |
+| Modules hide important design decisions | Pass | Core owns transitions, the collector owns output retention, and workspace/resource modules own cleanup. The collector's local Option pair remains a smaller improvement within that boundary. |
+| Module boundaries are discoverable | Pass | The architecture/runtime/selection/session concepts describe responsibilities and link their implementation evidence. |
+| 10–20% design-investment allocation | Unmeasured | Actual time allocation was not collected; no point awarded. |
+
+One Top representation addresses the failed locality row. Keeping Result in the
+collector reduces a local invariant without introducing another abstraction.
+An evidenced 10/10 would also require actual investment measurements; a code
+change cannot establish that criterion. No broad lifecycle enum or new generic
+state-management framework is recommended.
+
+# Findings
+
+## One normalized ranked request
+
+**Issue: [#959](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/959).** `VerifySelection` has Top and TopRange with the same
+count/policy. The parser distinguishes omitted offset from explicit zero; plan
+resolution and preview erase that distinction. The original fixed-batch design
+explicitly retained Top to preserve existing Rust construction. That requirement
+no longer applies. The recurring cost is synchronizing two variants and parser
+arms, then repeating normalization in consumers.[^post958-cli][^post958-plan]
+[^post958-preview][^post958-compat-intent]
+
+Use `Top { count, policy, offset }`, defaulting offset to zero at the parsing
+boundary. Retain raw optional input and clap constraints so illegal offset-only
+requests remain invalid. Remove TopRange, the extra constructor arm, the inner
+resolution match and the preview arm. Update Rust callers directly, with no
+alias or adapter. Preserve ranked order, retained-plan suffix clipping,
+max-mutants rejection, empty-plan error precedence and preview metadata.
+This does not change the ranking algorithm or saved formats.[^post958-plan]
+
+Implementation validation should compare omitted and explicit-zero offset,
+exercise all three policies and boundary offsets, retain the selection proptests
+and existing Lean paging correspondence tests, and compile all constructors.
+No new model is warranted for changing only the request representation.
+
+## Keep sink-or-error ownership in Result
+
+**Issue: [#960](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/960).** `collect_output_with_sink` receives `io::Result<S>` and
+splits it into two Options. During reception, successful sink ownership and
+first-error ownership are mutually exclusive, but each failed write must update
+both stores. Preserve the existing Result throughout reception and extract the
+sink only after EOF. This removes synchronized assignments and repeated
+first-error checks within one function.[^post958-output]
+
+The exclusivity claim applies to reception: current finalization failure can
+leave both a sink and error present before returning. No current invalid
+production transition was found. The proposed representation must still drain
+all chunks after create/write failure, count their bytes, drop a failed sink
+immediately, preserve the first error and avoid finalizing a failed sink.
+Early `?` during reception would violate the contract. Successful finalization
+runs once after EOF and its error must propagate.[^post958-output]
+
+The existing bounded-channel fault cases and output-retention corpus are relevant
+implementation gates. Add a focused finalization-failure behavioral case if
+existing coverage cannot observe that path. Use AAA and one concern per test;
+parameterize finite fault cases, reusing existing oracle inputs. Additional
+proptest coverage should address an actual uncovered input property. No new
+Lean proof or sink framework is warranted for this representation change.
+
+# Rejected candidates and current reasons
+
+The following candidates were reconsidered against current production flows,
+not rejected merely because an earlier audit rejected them.
+
+| Candidate | Current evidence and decision |
+| --- | --- |
+| Output continuation and cleanup classification from #955/#956 | PendingCompletion now owns output continuation; CleanupDisposition owns cleanup classification. Report-only evidence and report-started history have different jobs. Do not reopen resolved duplication. [^post958-machine][^post958-owned][^post958-shell] |
+| Core pending, run flags and session options | Completion ownership, active worker identity, cleanup reservation, attempted finish and observed outcomes have different lifetimes. A global state enum would combine independent axes. [^post958-machine] |
+| Core analysis queue and pending analysis | They coordinate at a narrow producer/consumer boundary. Bundling them is possible but no worthwhile independent change amplification was established. [^post958-machine] |
+| Shell in-flight counts and JoinSet | Counts represent unaccepted completions while task handles represent running/joinable tasks; task completion can precede event acceptance. Do not derive one from the other. [^post958-shell] |
+| Process output result and quiescence | A collector I/O error can coexist with settled work; aborting async work may lack proof that blocking I/O ended. Error outcome and cleanup evidence must remain distinct. [^post958-process] |
+| Admission gate and cancellation | They use similar synchronization, but prohibit new work and stop existing work respectively. An extraction would retain typed wrappers; no material net simplification was established. [^post958-process][^post958-shell] |
+| Resource closed and cleaned/terminated | Admission closes before cleanup, and failed cleanup must permit a retry. Windows notification consumption also differs from observed process exit. [^post958-linux][^post958-windows] |
+| Monitor handle and join status | Taking a handle transfers it to a blocking join; timeout can leave it running. Absence of the handle is not evidence of completed join. [^post958-disk] |
+| Managed-root flags and namespace states | Admission closure, durable readiness, live child ownership and rename/removal progression are independent evidence. Lock contention and partial failure produce valid combinations. [^post958-owned] |
+| Workspace registry and plan charges | Prepared blocking tasks temporarily own workspaces outside the registry; accounting must remain charged. Retention policy differs from successful physical cleanup. [^post958-workspace] |
+| Session finished and complete | Both complete and incomplete runs can be finalized. Real-process oracles read finished; releasing a lock does not distinguish finalization from a crash. [^post958-session][^post958-schema][^post958-lifecycle-oracle] |
+| Version and corruption checks | Schema 5 is accepted, empty stores initialized and other schemas rejected. Rechecking inside the transaction protects concurrent initialization. Fingerprint versions are a separate boundary. No old-format conversion ladder remains here. [^post958-schema][^post958-session] |
+| Shared report reader | Generic configuration preserves shared structural validation and current consumers' different needs. Optional fields represent current partial runs, not old-field repair. [^post958-report][^post958-machine] |
+| Progress projections and state | Latest state and consecutive stalls are distinct. A pair-only enum would duplicate most existing variants for one private producer. No useful net reduction demonstrated. [^post958-progress] |
+| Raw config and SessionMode | Optional path/resume represent unvalidated input; SessionMode already normalizes enabled ownership and intent. A second CLI-specific normalized type would duplicate conversion. [^post958-config][^post958-cli] |
+| Analyzer recording and control flow | Retained diagnostics, callback delivery and revisit suppression represent distinct behavior. Bounded heap, identity lookup and discarded-arrival evidence also serve different purposes. [^post958-analyzer] |
+| Prepared plans and review history | Immutable selection/provenance projections differ from executable choice; review history remains meaningful when no current row is applicable. No duplicate mutable authority demonstrated. [^post958-plan][^post958-review] |
+| Git Legacy mode | The fallback handles current rename/copy/unmerged/exceptional paths; removing it changes global patch semantics. Its name alone does not establish obsolete support. [^post958-git] |
+| all_legacy operator cohort | It supplies current defaults and historical fixture selection. Relocating its list retains the same knowledge elsewhere; no worthwhile simplification established. [^post958-config] |
+| Python import fallback | Direct-script invocation remains in release workflows while module imports serve tests. Standardizing invocations is possible, but the few branches do not justify a broad coordinated change in this audit. [^post958-release][^post958-release-workflow] |
+
+# Review and publication record
+
+Each domain reviewer recorded five separate passes. The coordinator also performed
+five passes at each stage; these are distinct checks, not repeated readings of
+the same diff.
+
+| Stage | Pass | Check and outcome |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Investigation | 1 | Pinned main after #957/#958, checked branch/remotes and isolated audit documentation. |
+| Investigation | 2 | Traced producers and consumers of state, including EOF and partial-cleanup transitions. |
+| Investigation | 3 | Distinguished current boundaries from historical compatibility using the explicit Top rationale. |
+| Investigation | 4 | Checked failure, timeout, retry and ownership-transfer counterexamples before proposing enums. |
+| Investigation | 5 | Compared replacement cost and existing tests; retained two bounded deletions and rejected broad rewrites. |
+| Proposal | 1 | Verified omitted offset equals zero after parsing; retained raw argument validation. |
+| Proposal | 2 | Corrected an initial draft: max-mutants rejects oversize selections; it does not truncate ranked requests. |
+| Proposal | 3 | Restricted sink/error exclusivity to reception and preserved EOF draining, sink drop and finalize errors. |
+| Proposal | 4 | Removed an unsupported implication that retention already has proptests; cited actual corpus tests and conditional additional coverage. |
+| Proposal | 5 | Independent reviewer challenged both drafts, their value, source links and validation scope. |
+| Publication | 1 | Rechecked open Issues and searched historical TopRange and collect_output_with_sink findings; #631 concerns allocation, not request representation. |
+| Publication | 2 | Checked immutable source locations and same-revision source hashes. |
+| Publication | 3 | Checked Japanese bodies for factual scope, low priority, no runtime defect claim and no adapter requirement. |
+| Publication | 4 | Created #959 and #960; retrieved both OPEN Issues and compared their full bodies byte-for-byte with the reviewed drafts. |
+| Publication | 5 | Checked structure, local links, reachability and 24 current-source hashes across 50 knowledge pages; strict site build and final diff review are recorded below. |
+
+Documentation validation on 2026-10-16 passed: the OKF checker inspected 50
+pages for structure, local links and reachability, checked source IDs/footnotes
+in the changed concepts, and verified all 24 current-source SHA-256 values.
+`python -m tools.docs_site --output site` completed with MkDocs strict mode.
+`git diff --check` passed. These are documentation checks, not runtime evidence.
+
+# Limits and recheck triggers
+
+This audit does not establish runtime correctness on macOS, Linux or Windows,
+performance, arbitrary interleavings or exhaustive absence of compatibility
+code. New tests and proofs belong to implementation work when behavior changes
+or existing evidence does not cover a changed path. Recheck these findings when
+ranked-request semantics, collector error handling, cleanup ownership, artifact
+versions or release entry points change. Open issue status was checked on
+2026-10-16; Issue references track the proposals after this static snapshot.
